Review measurement: binary hook subagent-start 1.93 s, --version 1.57 s (sha256 0.2 s), Python hook 0.12 s, Perl source 0.5 s. Two hooks per spawn. Decide before pinning checksums whether to activate the binary hook path or keep Python for spawn hooks.

k6 measurement, Linux x86_64 (2026-11-18, local, shared host, load 1.2-3.4). Binary: probe asset of be2b19d through the k7 starter bin/briefing with a fixture pin manifest (test gate). Wall clock per process, milliseconds, median (min-max):
- starter -> cached binary, hook subagent-start, warm (n=20): 860 (684-1608)
- starter -> cached binary, hook pre-tool-use, warm (n=20): 863 (686-2176)
- starter -> cached binary, subagent-start, PAR tree removed (n=5): 5298 (4865-5878)
- binary started directly, warm (n=20): 873 (609-1744)
- sha256sum of the cached binary, the starter's per-call rehash (n=20): 150 (124-192)
- Python hook hooks/briefing-preload direct, subagent-start (n=20): 82 (77-102); pre-tool-use: 90 (76-192)
- starter -> Python fallback (no pins), subagent-start: 203 (180-235); pre-tool-use: 196 (98-246)
- cold provision hook session-start (file:// fixture: copy, hash, install, warm-up, n=3): 3386 (2742-4279)
Local chain test runs by the reviewer on the same host: warm 928-980, Python 65-143, cold PAR 1813-3447.
Native chain on GitHub runners (binaries probe 44244389169, asset of 21917f2, tests/test_binary_chain.py chain_timing medians): linux-x86_64 (ubuntu-24.04) warm subagent-start 134.5 (n=10, 133.9-138.7), cold PAR 350.4 (n=3), cold provision 493.4 (n=3), Python hook 21.3 (n=10); linux-arm64 (ubuntu-24.04-arm) warm 187.4, cold PAR 635.0, cold provision 809.8, Python 31.2. The shared local host is 4-15x slower across the board; the runner numbers are the cleaner reference, the ratio binary/Python is ~6x on both runners.
Decomposition (Perl source, best of 5): require App::Briefing::CLI 270 ms, of which TOML::Tiny::Parser alone 168 ms (lib/App/Briefing.pm:10 loads it unconditionally; only Codex TOML agents need it); Moo 30, Path::Tiny 48, JSON::MaybeXS 37, namespace::clean 39. So a warm call is ~150 ms rehash (required by the design) + ~270 ms module compile + PAR start-up + the hook itself.
Output check: binary and Python outputs parse to identical JSON.
Recommendation (decision Getty's): load TOML::Tiny lazily where TOML agents are parsed (168 ms of a 860 ms warm call on the local host, ~20%; proportionally ~25-35 ms on the runners). It must land before dzil release 0.001 if the first pinned binaries should have it, because the binaries are built from the release tarball. Even then a spawn hook through the binary stays several times slower than the Python hook (runner: ~0.11 s vs ~0.02 s); Python remains the faster path wherever it exists. macOS and Windows numbers follow from the native chain run once those legs are green.

macOS note (k7 native round, review M5): with the macOS path exec the starter hashes the cached binary twice per start (once when preparing, once right before the exec). Deliberate for the swap check; it adds one openssl SHA-256 of the binary to every macOS spawn hook.

Native chain numbers, round 2 (binaries probe 44259077774 attempt 2, asset of 4cac15b, tests/test_binary_chain.py chain_timing, milliseconds, median of n=10 warm / n=3 cold):
- linux-x86_64: warm subagent-start 236.8, cold PAR 663.6, cold provision 910.9, Python hook 36.6
