- macos-x86_64 (macos-26-intel): warm 663.5, cold PAR 2200.8, cold provision 2691.9, Python 87.9
- windows-x86_64: cold provision 5472.4 only; warm calls fail natively in this round (cache-hit bug under repair), so no warm/cold-PAR/Python numbers yet.
Runner hardware varies between runs (linux-x86_64 warm was 134.5 in round 1, 236.8 now), so compare within one run: the binary/Python ratio is 6.3-7.5x on every platform measured. The macOS warm path hashes twice (M5 note above), included in these numbers.
Recommendation unchanged (decision Getty's): lazy TOML::Tiny before dzil release 0.001; the binary stays the slower path wherever Python exists. Windows numbers follow once the warm path is green.

Native chain numbers, round 3 (binaries probe 44268575472, asset of f906a81, all five chain legs green; milliseconds, median of n=10 warm / n=3 cold, file:// fixture for the provision):
- linux-x86_64: warm subagent-start 137.8, cold PAR 352.5, cold provision 507.6, Python hook 21.1 (6.5x)
- linux-arm64: warm 174.8, cold PAR 586.1, cold provision 754.3, Python 27.8 (6.3x)
- macos-arm64: warm 356.4, cold PAR 893.2, cold provision 1284.4, Python 59.5 (6.0x)
- macos-x86_64: warm 570.6, cold PAR 2184.9, cold provision 2652.6, Python 73.7 (7.7x)
- windows-x86_64 (windows-2022): warm 948.5, cold PAR 3691.8, cold provision 5464.7, Python 43.9 (21.6x)
Windows is the outlier: about 0.95 s per spawn hook through bin/briefing.exe -> briefing-bootstrap.exe -> verified PAR, against 0.04 s for the Python hook. That run does not split the cost between the per-call rehash, PAR start-up and real-time scanning of a freshly started exe; a per-phase measurement on Windows would.
Every cold provision is far below the 75 s gate, but these runs copy from a file:// fixture. The network download is measured only with real release assets (Task 10 step 4).
Recommendation (decision Getty's):
(1) Load TOML::Tiny lazily before dzil release 0.001, as noted above.
(2) Windows: keep the Python hook as the first choice where a real Python exists, and use the binary only as the no-Python path, at least until the warm cost is understood. This changes which program users run, which is outside the approved design, so it needs Getty's ruling; the k7 starters are unchanged.
(3) POSIX: with 0.14-0.57 s per spawn hook the binary is acceptable as the no-Python path; Python stays faster wherever it exists.

